# Target C — exact Type-II five-currency conservation

**Date:** 2026-09-12  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**  
**Status:** proof-only structural composition; theorem authority unchanged.

This note combines the audited Type-II loaded-hull identities with the audited
component remainder of §§160–161.  The result is an exact finite conservation law
linking the harmonic-diameter moment, component non-geodesicity, global bounded-face
defect, outer-walk surplus, and diameter/rank topology.

No source point is removed and no contact-isolated coordinate enters a core rigidity
statement.

## 1. Notation

Use the current Type-II notation

- `q=|B|` for the loaded force-hull vertex count;
- `m` for the number of nonunit sides of the loaded force hull `F`;
- `Q` for global bounded nonquadrilateral face degree excess;
- `k=d_H+s_Z`;
- `u_B=x_B-m>=0` for the outer-walk occurrence surplus beyond the one-extra-edge
  baseline on nonunit hull sides;
- `d_r=diam(P_r)` for harmonic attachment cells;
- `G_D` for the exact component remainder of §160;
- `b` for the old outer contact-walk occurrence length; and
- `P_F=per(F)`.

The centralized audited Type-II hull-side theorem gives

\[
 e_{BB}=q-m=Q+2k+u_B.
\tag{1.1}
\]

Audited §160 gives

\[
 G_D=2+q+m-k-\sum_r d_r.
\tag{1.2}
\]

Both identities are finite.

## 2. Five-currency conservation

From (1.1),

\[
 Q+u_B=q-m-2k.
\tag{2.1}
\]

Add (2.1), `k`, and `sum_r d_r+G_D`.  Using (1.2),

\[
\begin{aligned}
\sum_r d_r+G_D+Q+u_B+k
&=(2+q+m-k)+(q-m-2k)+k\\
&=2+2q-2k.
\end{aligned}
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{
 2(q-k)+2
 =\sum_r d_r+G_D+Q+u_B+k.
}
\tag{2.2}
\]

Every term on the right except the harmless constant is an existing geometric or
combinatorial currency.  In particular, (2.2) is an equality, not a relaxation.

Two immediate exact rearrangements are

\[
\boxed{
 Q+u_B
 =2(q-k)+2-k-G_D-\sum_r d_r,
}
\tag{2.3}
\]

and

\[
\boxed{
 \sum_r d_r+G_D
 =2(q-k)+2-k-Q-u_B.
}
\tag{2.4}
\]

Thus a large harmonic-diameter/remainder profile and a large boundary-defect profile
cannot be spent independently.

## 3. Exact outer-detour identity

The loaded-hull outer-walk ledger has

\[
 b=q+x_B=q+m+u_B.
\tag{3.1}
\]

Solving (1.2) for `q+m` and substituting in (3.1) gives

\[
 b
 =\sum_r d_r+G_D+k+u_B-2.
\tag{3.2}
\]

Hence

\[
\boxed{
 b-P_F
 =\sum_r d_r+G_D+k+u_B-P_F-2.
}
\tag{3.3}
\]

This identifies precisely how a large harmonic-diameter moment can coexist with an
almost-geodesic old outer walk: it must be offset by the force-hull perimeter together
with the component/topology/surplus currencies on the right.

Define the hull-side chord currency

\[
 \chi_D:=q+m-P_F.
\tag{3.4}
\]

Then

\[
\boxed{
 b-P_F=u_B+\chi_D.
}
\tag{3.5}
\]

If the nonunit hull sides have lengths `s_i`, then, because the other `q-m` hull sides
are unit sides,

\[
\boxed{
 \chi_D=\sum_{i:\,s_i>1}(2-s_i).
}
\tag{3.6}
\]

The sign is not fixed: two-edge tooth bases contribute positively, while nonunit hull
sides longer than two contribute negatively.  Equation (3.5), not `chi_D>=0`, is the
safe statement.  In particular `chi_D<0` forces `u_B>=-chi_D` because the geometric
outer detour is nonnegative.

## 4. Refinement of the stopped-train phase elimination

Let `T` be the complete stopped-train count and `f` the bounded nonquadrilateral face
count.  The exact train ledger is

\[
 T=q+2f-k.
\tag{4.1}
\]

Since every bounded nonquadrilateral face contributes at least one unit to `Q`,
`f<=Q`.  Therefore

\[
\begin{aligned}
T-{q\over2}
&={q\over2}+2f-k\\
&\le {q\over2}+2Q-k\\
&\le {q\over2}+2(Q+u_B)-k.
\end{aligned}
\tag{4.2}
\]

From (1.1) and (3.4),

\[
\begin{aligned}
Q+u_B
&=q-m-2k\\
&=2(q-k)-P_F-\chi_D.
\end{aligned}
\tag{4.3}
\]

Insert (4.3) in (4.2) and rearrange.  This gives the finite inequality

\[
\boxed{
q-k
\ge
{2\over9}\left(T-{q\over2}\right)
+{1\over9}k
+{4\over9}(P_F+\chi_D).
}
\tag{4.4}
\]

Equivalently, since `P_F+chi_D=q+m`,

\[
 q-k
\ge
{2\over9}\left(T-{q\over2}\right)
+{1\over9}k
+{4\over9}(q+m).
\tag{4.5}
\]

The form (4.4) exposes the geometry lost when one replaces the actual hull by the
coarse Type-II perimeter limit.

The audited all-regime atomic-gap theorem supplies an absolute `eta_grid>0` with

\[
{T-q/2\over D}
\ge
2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+\eta_{grid}-o(1),
\qquad
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3}.
\tag{4.6}
\]

Type II has `P_F/D->pi`.  Thus every convergent Type-II subsequence satisfies

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {q-k\over D}
\ge
C_{146}+{2\over9}\eta_{grid}
+{1\over9}\liminf{k\over D}
+{4\over9}\liminf{\chi_D\over D},
}
\tag{4.7}
\]

whenever the displayed liminf terms are taken on one common subsequence, where

\[
 C_{146}={4\over9}\left(\pi+\sqrt{\Delta_2}\right).
\]

More safely, without separating liminfs, (4.4) gives along every subsequence

\[
\boxed{
{q-k\over D}
-{k\over9D}
-{4\chi_D\over9D}
\ge
C_{146}+{2\over9}\eta_{grid}-o(1).
}
\tag{4.8}
\]

Thus the only way the hull-side correction can weaken the perimeter-only §146 floor is
through a linear population of long nonunit hull sides; by (3.5) that escape forces a
matching amount of outer-walk occurrence surplus.

## 5. Scope and frontier

The identities (2.2) and (3.3) are exact finite Type-II statements once the audited
`c_B=0` theorem and §160 remainder are assumed.  The phase refinement (4.8) additionally
uses the audited all-regime atomic-gap theorem.

This does **not** close Type II.  The unresolved escape is explicit:

```text
large harmonic/profile currency
  -> five-currency conservation
  -> either stronger q-k pressure
     or long nonunit hull sides / outer-walk surplus.
```

A closing continuation should therefore attack the negative `chi_D` branch by showing
that linear long-side excess cannot be carried by the near-disk outer contact routes
without additional bounded-face, phase-order, or source-diameter cost.

**Disposition:** new proof-only structural invariant and correlated elimination; C-II
remains open.
